			<div class="content" id="post-content-4858274">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Gonna be honest: I don't really vibe with this. It feels&#8230;like a kinda stereotypical 'descent into madness' kinda story without really doing much to like, distinguish itself? Like it feels reminiscent of something like <a href="/scp-3000">SCP-3000</a> or <a href="/scp-4391">SCP-4391</a> but in a much more restrained way than the second and without the narrative heft of the first. In some ways, the uncertainty of the description of the SCP feels reminiscent of stuff like <a href="/scp-252">SCP-252</a> or a lot of antimemetic related stuff.</p>
<p>Beyond this, the way the narrative plays out feels&#8230;iunno, I think having it be framed as transcripts from the last surviving agent will really kill all the tension because we'll immediately know that everything the people do to try to survive will be futile and so it might be really hard to capture a readers interest. You might be able to manage a kinda, deep dread here where like, because we know it's futile, we just, are faced with the inevitability of the end; but it feels like that'd be really hard to do well.</p>
<p>On a very minor note, I have no idea how 'Midnight Sun' ties in here as a name.</p>
<hr />
<p>Normally I'd offer some advice on how to improve this but I just, generally think it's not hugely something that appeals to me so you might find other reviewers are more receptive to it; or you might find that like, really leaning into the weirdness with it more, or like, doing more with it than just 'everyone ends up dying' would help?</p>
</div>
